※ pl4g-test: compile=ok run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ A type definition is `type NAME = ` and then a sequence of `NAME : TYPE`
※ pairs.  The separator says which kind of type it is: `;` for a product,
※ which holds all of its parts at once, and `|` for a sum, which holds exactly
※ one of them.  Those are the two characters that already mean "and also" and
※ "or else" everywhere else in the language.
※
※ The sequence may be written over several lines in either of the two ways the
※ language already breaks a line -- inside braces, or indented under the
※ definition -- and neither changes what separates the pairs.

※ one line, one pair: a record of one field, since a choice between one
※ alternative is not a thing worth writing
type Handle = value : u64

※ one line, several pairs
type Point = x : f64 ; y : f64

※ inside braces
type Span = { first : u32 ; last : u32 }

※ indented, with the separator ending each line
type Line =
    from : Point ;
    to : Point

※ a sum, whose variants may carry nothing: which is what an enumeration is
type Colour =
    red : void |
    green : void |
    blue : void

※ a sum whose variants carry something, written inside braces
type Number = { whole : i64 | fraction : f64 }

※ a type may name another written below it, and may be a result
type Outer = inner : Inner ; maybe : u8?
type Inner = a : u8

@[startup]
fn main() → u8:
    0u8
